[
  "ACT 1: COLD OPEN & PERSONAL GROUNDING [00:00 - 03:00]\nScene: 01. Starting Soon -> 02. Monologue / Full Camera\n\nWelcome back to The Sound Above. I am Mike Hall. Today we enter The AI Horizon & The Observable Control Plane. Before code synthesis models and automated agents, developers met face to face to establish standards of craftsmanship. Today we rewatch Episode 29: Jason Cranford Teague from WebVisions 2013 (2013).",
  "ACT 2: SETTING THE HISTORICAL SCENE [03:00 - 08:00]\nScene: 02. Monologue / Full Camera\n\nJason's WebVisions conversation captured the transition from print publishing to living web platforms.",
  "ACT 3: THE CORE INQUIRY: THE SOUND ABOVE [08:00 - 14:00]\nScene: 02. Monologue / Full Camera -> 04. Split Screen\n\nWhat happened to the physical technical book? How does knowledge preservation change when documentation becomes dynamic and digital? What principles guided Jason Cranford Teague when this conversation was recorded? How do those lessons inform the software systems we design today?",
  "ACT 4: HALLWAY REWATCH & VIDEO REACTION [14:00 - 28:00]\nScene: 03. Reaction - Video Focus\n\nRoll archive footage (YouTube ID: 0RBob_r4rGk). Watch for the unvarnished hallway exchanges, the emphasis on craft over speed, and the sheer enthusiasm of engineers taking responsibility for their software.",
  "ACT 5: THE AI-ERA PARALLEL & TERMINAL WORKBENCH [28:00 - 40:00]\nScene: 05. Workbench - Code & Terminal\n\nIn an era of disposable synthetic code, living documentation and verified provenance are the anchors that prevent historical amnesia. When implementation is cheap, verification and clarity become everything. We cannot accept the mirage of competence. Let us examine the terminal contracts directly.",
  "ACT 6: COMMUNITY CODA, OUTRO & NEXT STREAM [40:00 - 45:00]\nScene: 08. Outro & Next Stream\n\nThank you for joining today's session. Full transcripts, provenance notes, and companion research are available at just3ws.com. In our next broadcast, we explore Episode 30. Until next time, keep your feedback loops fast and your code humane."
]